{“question”: “Under the responsible-AI standards chapter on Robustness, why does continuous retraining on live operational data expose a machine learning system to poisoning attacks?”, “answer”: “Retraining requires a continuous way to collect live data with labels. Because systems can be retrained on data gathered during operation, an attacker can inject malicious samples during operation that later disrupt or influence retraining.”, “evidence”: [{“page”: 2, “quote”: “To do so, you should have put in place a continuous way to collect live data, with their labels, which is often not the case.”}, {“page”: 4, “quote”: “As ML systems can be re-trained using data collected during operation, an attacker may poison the data by injecting malicious samples during operation, which subsequently disrupt or influence re-training.”}]},
{“question”: “The responsible-AI standards chapter on Robustness list causes of data skew. Besides training design, unavailable production features, data dependencies and upstream changes, what final cause is given?”, “answer”: “Data integrity: inconsistencies that go unnoticed in deployed AI systems. Teams need to detect feature errors such as missing values, type mismatches and range anomalies.”, “evidence”: [{“page”: 1, “quote”: “Data skew occurs when the Machine Learning model is trained on the data that does not represent the live data.”}, {“page”: 2, “quote”: “· Data Integrity: Data inconsistencies can often go unnoticed in deployed Al systems.”}]},
